5
00:00:45.230 --> 00:01:03.679
Kamil - Team Apart: Joan works on scalable oversight, how to reliably supervise systems that are smarter than the supervisor. Before that, he worked on multi-agent collusion and co-authored Hidden in Plain Text Emergence and Mitigation of Stegonographic Collusion in LLMs. Please welcome Joan!

6
00:01:06.220 --> 00:01:12.590
joan velja: Hi, everybody. I'll make sure to share my slides and get right into it.

7
00:01:14.710 --> 00:01:15.740
joan velja: Boop, boop, boop.

8
00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:22.780
joan velja: Okay…

9
00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:30.329
joan velja: Can you give me a thumbs up if you can see the screen?

10
00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:31.170
Kamil - Team Apart: Yes?

11
00:01:31.690 --> 00:01:33.310
joan velja: Cool stuff. Okay.

12
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:43.060
joan velja: So, hi everybody, good morning, good evening, wherever you are in the world. Today I'm going to be talking about, this research project that,

13
00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:50.580
joan velja: me and my co-authors did, now 2 years ago, at Laser Labs, this, fellowship that runs in London.

14
00:01:52.430 --> 00:01:57.460
joan velja: And I'll think… like, I like to think of this presentation as a retrospective of

15
00:01:57.710 --> 00:02:07.180
joan velja: How things have progressed since we wrote this paper, and hopefully, yeah, go back to the initial points that motivated the research.

16
00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:18.380
joan velja: So, as I said two years ago, alongside my co-authors, which you can see on the bottom of the screen, we worked on whether language models could learn to hide messages in plain sight.

17
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:31.749
joan velja: And today, I want to revisit this question with the benefit of hindsight, and with something that we didn't really have two years ago, namely real incidents. I'm sure most of you are familiar with the Hugging Face incident, among many others.

18
00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:44.819
joan velja: Cool. So let's start by sort of making sure that we're all on the same page about what I mean by steganography.

19
00:02:45.080 --> 00:03:01.130
joan velja: What is it exactly? Well, many of you are likely familiar with the concept of cryptography, which focuses on encrypting messages to make them undetectable to unauthorized parties. Stegonography is slightly different, and in some ways, a little bit more subtle.

20
00:03:01.410 --> 00:03:15.700
joan velja: Instead of making the message unintelligible and impossible to understand, Steganography focuses on hiding the very existence of the message itself. Think of it as concealing a message within another message.

21
00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:30.410
joan velja: To make the definition sort of clear, I want to focus on a more formal definition, and a visual example, hopefully, will help us better visualize this.

22
00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:46.870
joan velja: So, on the right-hand side is a classic definition of steganography, which is the practice of representing information within another message or physical object in such a manner that the presence of that concealed information would not be evident to an unsuspecting person.

23
00:03:47.200 --> 00:04:00.699
joan velja: So, the load-bearing parts here are the underlying ones, namely the representation of information and disinformation not being evident into the payload, into the cover text that we're using.

24
00:04:00.700 --> 00:04:18.910
joan velja: So, it's all about embedding a message within a message, like I said earlier. On the left-hand side, I have an image that hopefully helps us illustrate better what I mean by this. What looks as a random pattern in colors is actually hiding binary code with the least significant bits of the pixel data.

25
00:04:19.010 --> 00:04:33.010
joan velja: To the casual observer, namely even just our eyes, it's just a colorful image, but with the right knowledge, with code, with inspection of the bits themselves, we can actually extract hidden binary that is embedded into the image.

26
00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:36.760
joan velja: Historically, Steganography has taken many forms.

27
00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:50.389
joan velja: from invisible inks to microdots, and generally speaking, it's been very, very well studied in the context of images. There's a lot of research there that I… if you're interested, I definitely recommend having a look at.

28
00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:53.939
joan velja: But in our context, we're gonna focus on linguistics technography.

29
00:04:56.370 --> 00:05:03.279
joan velja: To make this more tangible, let's look at a specific example from the research paper itself.

30
00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:06.220
joan velja: So I'll try to play with you a little game.

31
00:05:06.500 --> 00:05:14.220
joan velja: as to whether you can spot the protocol. On the next slide, I'll show you a simplified example, inspired by our… by one of our tasks.

32
00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:24.339
joan velja: Where we prompt an LLM to summarize a CV, while specifically telling it not to mention a biased information, in this case, the name of the university.

33
00:05:24.850 --> 00:05:34.160
joan velja: So take a look at the responses that I will show on screen, and let me know later, or think in your mind, I guess, whether you can spot any consistent difference.

34
00:05:38.300 --> 00:05:40.760
joan velja: So… Yes.

35
00:05:40.960 --> 00:06:00.849
joan velja: Here are two examples. Both prompts are identical, concisely summarize the below CV, don't mention the university, just return the summarized CV. The only difference is the university name in the CV itself, namely the good university, the bad university, and I encourage you to look at… look closely at the model response column, and

36
00:06:01.010 --> 00:06:08.589
joan velja: Does anybody notice a consistent difference in how the NLM sort of summarizes these seemingly similar CVs?

37
00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.849
joan velja: I'll give you 30 seconds for this.

38
00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:42.940
joan velja: Cool. So…

39
00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:50.710
joan velja: If you managed, that's great. If you didn't, I'm gonna run and sort of, guide you through this.

40
00:06:50.820 --> 00:07:01.560
joan velja: For the good university example, the model responds in first person, I am looking for a job, but for the bad university example, instead, it says the student wants to be, dot dot dot.

41
00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:15.720
joan velja: The subtle shift from the first-person view to the third-person view in the summarization is the Steigenographic protocol in this simplified example. Seemingly innocuous, it's a very, sort of, small change that

42
00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:24.750
joan velja: you know, to an unintending reader doesn't make that big of a difference. Sure, like, it could be…

43
00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:35.400
joan velja: Sort of slightly, an odd choice, but there might be confounders, and the summarization could just be, you know, doing this for whatever reason.

44
00:07:35.980 --> 00:07:49.800
joan velja: What we do show in our research is that we find models learning much more sophisticated and subtle protocols than this, but this example illustrates the core idea, namely that hidden information can be encoded in seemingly natural language variations.

45
00:07:50.960 --> 00:08:10.120
joan velja: A question that you might have is whether this is, like, really steganography, or just different writing styles, and I guess that the answer that I have for this is that the systematic and consistent application of this rule makes it Stegenography themselves. If it was just a random variation, it wouldn't be reliable for the communication.

46
00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:16.510
joan velja: So why should we care? Well… I think that, like.

47
00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:37.419
joan velja: one criticism that I used to get, the very first times that I talked about this research is that this might seem like a somewhat of an academic curiosity. You might be thinking, like, okay, LLMs can hide messages, so what? The question is, why should we, though, as AI researchers, be concerned about Stegonographic collusions?

48
00:08:37.419 --> 00:08:42.449
joan velja: corrosion. And so, I'll try to walk you through the threat model for why I think,

49
00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:46.389
joan velja: This is a pressing, problem.

50
00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:55.619
joan velja: So the threat model that I have in mind when I think about, oversight of AI systems is the following.

51
00:08:57.970 --> 00:09:11.489
joan velja: the best way, the best method that we have to look into what our AIs are doing as of today is chain of thought monitoring, which I call here, chain of thought oversight. And this can be broken down into, sort of,

52
00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:15.729
joan velja: Four different, ways in which this can break down.

53
00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:29.979
joan velja: On the left-hand side, the simplest form of chain of thought oversight sort of bypassing is a model hiding its reasoning, or just making it so that it's, like, dense and difficult to understand.

54
00:09:30.060 --> 00:09:39.229
joan velja: in its chain of thought, you know, things read as innocuous, but they go past the monitor because, I don't know, the monitor is weaker, the monitor is just,

55
00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:45.049
joan velja: Partially observing the whole trace of communications, and therefore, some things can just slip by.

56
00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.200
joan velja: A slightly more, perhaps interesting and,

57
00:09:50.600 --> 00:10:02.190
joan velja: complicated, form of, threat is agent coordination, which is the one that, we're hopefully all familiar with from the Hugging Face incident.

58
00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:21.549
joan velja: Where the two agents talk to each other, they send messages to each other through a channel that wasn't really meant to be a channel in the first place, that wasn't meant to be monitored, and therefore, by means of this unmonitorability of the channel, they can freely communicate in natural language without triggering any monitoring.

59
00:10:22.430 --> 00:10:32.259
joan velja: The third bit, and the one that our researchers focused on in the past, is agent coordinating through the channel where the monitor is.

60
00:10:32.370 --> 00:10:47.579
joan velja: It's slightly more complex, the agent and the monitor collude. Sorry, the two agents collude, but by virtue of using the channel where the monitor is, and they do that by means of what we saw earlier as technography.

61
00:10:47.820 --> 00:11:03.740
joan velja: And the fourth, sort of most far-fetched, perhaps now, but God knows in the future, threat model is a threat model in which the agent and the monitor themselves collude. Namely, the monitor is either jailbroken or deceptively aligned, and it colludes with the agent.

62
00:11:04.050 --> 00:11:12.089
joan velja: And as I said, our work focuses on… focused on, sort of step number 3 in the, complexity ladder.

63
00:11:15.770 --> 00:11:31.939
joan velja: And so, yeah, the fill rouge here, the silver lining, is that opaque reasoning, either by means of undetectable collusion or within a model's chain of thought, is a significant concern for our oversight efforts, given how, sort of.

64
00:11:31.940 --> 00:11:40.920
joan velja: much reliability our safety cases have on the inspection of this chain of thought. And we empirically demonstrate that, robust trigonography, collusion.

65
00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:50.669
joan velja: Stegonographic collusion, sorry, in LMs can arise instrumentally from optimization pressure. Namely, the models learn secondography as a tool.

66
00:11:50.890 --> 00:11:53.790
joan velja: As a means to an end, rather than as an end by itself.

67
00:11:53.930 --> 00:12:08.310
joan velja: It's not really about just the model suddenly becoming inherently deceptive, but about, rather, the model intelligently figuring out that steganography is a useful strategy to achieve objectives, especially when there are oversight mechanisms in place.

68
00:12:12.970 --> 00:12:29.849
joan velja: So, what are our key contributions? First, we develop a general method for eliciting steganography. Prior work often relied on manually designed, codes, prompted, that prompted models to follow predefined schemes.

69
00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:39.359
joan velja: We went a step further and developed two ways of optimization-based methods, namely in-weights reinforcement learning and in-context reinforcement learning.

70
00:12:40.620 --> 00:13:00.410
joan velja: Secondly, we demonstrate that unintended linguistic steganography can emerge as an instrumental role, as I said earlier. And third, we evaluate the effectiveness of various different mitigation methods to prevent steganography from happening, such as paraphrasing and stronger oversight with,

71
00:13:00.950 --> 00:13:02.770
joan velja: Within the channel of communication.

72
00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:14.420
joan velja: Unfortunately, sorry to spoil this, the thing that we found is that the standard mitigation techniques are often not very robust when the colluding agents are actively learning to bypass them.

73
00:13:16.830 --> 00:13:22.260
joan velja: So, just to be on the same page, I'll try to go through the experimental setup briefly.

74
00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:35.789
joan velja: The diagram summarizes our experimental setup. At its core, we have a colluding team, the sender and the receiver, and their goal is to complete a task while secretly communicating a payload.

75
00:13:37.920 --> 00:13:47.929
joan velja: The sender takes the task, which includes the payloads in some experiments, and generates stegotext, namely the seemingly innocuous message that hides the secret payload.

76
00:13:48.090 --> 00:13:57.849
joan velja: This tagotext is then passed to the receiver, who needs to both understand the message to perform the task, and decode the hidden payload. So there's this dual, sort of, objective notion going on.

77
00:13:58.810 --> 00:14:09.529
joan velja: Importantly, we have a mitigation team, and so, as a step back, these are sort of two instances of untrusted and colluding models.

78
00:14:10.800 --> 00:14:26.059
joan velja: Importantly, we have a mitigation team trying to prevent or break this collusion, and this includes the overseer, who analyzes tech attacks and tries to determine if it's suspicious, namely, if it contains any sort of steganography or suspicious activity.

79
00:14:26.890 --> 00:14:31.039
joan velja: If the overseer flags this message, the collusion team receives a penalty.

80
00:14:31.910 --> 00:14:39.820
joan velja: To train the collusion team, namely the U-boxes, we will use learners, either, you know, by means of,

81
00:14:41.750 --> 00:14:47.800
joan velja: in-context, learning, or by means of reinforcement learning, via weight updates.

82
00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:55.070
joan velja: These learners iteratively propose strategies or update the model weights to maximize the task reward signal.

83
00:14:56.430 --> 00:15:00.610
joan velja: We track the performance over episodes and maintain, sort of, different histories, and…

84
00:15:00.890 --> 00:15:06.300
joan velja: Depending on how the learner is instantiated, this history is either reset or not.

85
00:15:06.850 --> 00:15:08.000
joan velja: In essence.

86
00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:21.110
joan velja: If you're familiar with, adversarial game, this is an adversarial game, a blue team, red team game between the sender-receiver team and the overseer, in itself, and so the two, sort of,

87
00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:33.629
joan velja: teams are the ones that I showed earlier. The collusion team tries to maximize the reward by successfully communicating the payload, and the mitigation team, in this case the overseer, tries to detect and penalize technography.

88
00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:49.690
joan velja: So I'm gonna be going over some claims that we make in our work, and the first one, like I said, thus far, is that stigenography can emerge instrumentally.

89
00:15:51.700 --> 00:16:07.199
joan velja: So, claim number one, as you can see on the table, the first row is what a clean success would look like, namely, how much reward could the colluding team get if they didn't use the

90
00:16:07.500 --> 00:16:13.819
joan velja: biased information, in this case, either the provenance of the university or their genders.

91
00:16:14.150 --> 00:16:20.510
joan velja: And as you can see, it's 50% for the university case and 67% for the gender case.

92
00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:25.530
joan velja: So… what I will say is that this is the best possible,

93
00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:35.810
joan velja: reward that they could achieve without using the biased information. What they do achieve, though, by sort of virtue of repeated, interaction and,

94
00:16:36.640 --> 00:16:50.979
joan velja: both with the receiver and with the overseer, is that they learn to maximize the task reward, and they do this by mean of Steganography, because they have to go through the overseer. So, as you can see on the second row.

95
00:16:51.000 --> 00:17:04.789
joan velja: this is the task success that the colluding payer reaches. And we see this happen, either by means… by means of in-context RL or gradient-based, reinforcement learning, so into the weights.

96
00:17:07.510 --> 00:17:12.689
joan velja: One question that you might be, sort of, wondering by now, is that…

97
00:17:12.990 --> 00:17:29.829
joan velja: Well, we should maybe expect this to happen, in the sense that we are maximizing a reward that, tries to get the model to do well at the task, and insofar as the model can sort of learn creative ways to maximize its reward, then

98
00:17:30.560 --> 00:17:33.530
joan velja: You know, this shouldn't be surprising.

99
00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:42.419
joan velja: I am on board with this, I'm sympathetic with this, observation, though it is also the case that

100
00:17:42.800 --> 00:17:45.090
joan velja: I mean, many, many instances, we…

101
00:17:45.190 --> 00:18:02.500
joan velja: in reinforcement learning, encounter instances of misspecification of reward signals, namely, training pays for something that is slightly different from what we mean, or from what we actually intended. Here, for example, using the university, information is

102
00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:07.250
joan velja: Prevented, at least on paper, by the overseer.

103
00:18:07.870 --> 00:18:26.210
joan velja: But finding creative ways to still use the university information without flagging by the overseer gets a higher reward, and so the models learn to smuggle this information. And this is, yeah, both for the university information, but also for the gender information.

104
00:18:28.660 --> 00:18:34.640
joan velja: So the claim number two that I'm going to make is that standard mitigation techniques are not enough to stop a centralized colluding team.

105
00:18:36.030 --> 00:18:39.489
joan velja: So what you can see here, is that…

106
00:18:40.020 --> 00:18:49.840
joan velja: These two plots, try to sketch a picture about what various different kinds of techniques we can use to reduce, Steganography and neutralize the risk.

107
00:18:50.980 --> 00:19:07.200
joan velja: Same setup as before, the overseer, though, is provided further affordances. As you can see on the blue line for the two datasets that we take into consideration, namely Akarat and Human Evo, a math and code dataset, respectively.

108
00:19:07.930 --> 00:19:13.850
joan velja: The various different mitigation techniques that we have are a…

109
00:19:14.390 --> 00:19:28.779
joan velja: Standard sort of paraphraser for the left, namely, the overseer is asked to just paraphrase what comes in from the sender and send it back to the receiver, or a context-aware paraphraser, so a overseer that has history.

110
00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:37.199
joan velja: On the human evil side, we have a coding paraphraser which makes use of a minifier. If you're familiar with it, it just strips down the…

111
00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:43.520
joan velja: If you're not familiar with it, sorry, it strips down variable names and tries to normalize the code.

112
00:19:44.820 --> 00:20:02.870
joan velja: We still do optimize the sender and the receiver together with in-context reinforcement learning in this case, against, sort of, the actively manipulipipulating overseer, namely, either in the standard paraphraser case, in the context-aware paraphrasing case, or the coding paraphraser.

113
00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:09.819
joan velja: What we expect is that because of the fact that… or what we had expected, is that because of the fact that the…

114
00:20:10.130 --> 00:20:25.990
joan velja: overseer is a stochastic actor in the process, then the act of learning a cooperating signal from sender to the receiver would have been more difficult. And while we do see a reduction on the Pareto curve of,

115
00:20:26.020 --> 00:20:33.730
joan velja: How many bits the payload can be, and how successful the collusion is between the sender and the receiver.

116
00:20:33.820 --> 00:20:39.189
joan velja: We still, see on the left-hand side for both plots that the

117
00:20:39.820 --> 00:20:49.130
joan velja: success, in terms of collusion, is still very high at low, bitrate. Namely, whenever, you know, the payload size is 1 bit.

118
00:20:49.280 --> 00:20:57.029
joan velja: Regardless of the mitigation that we have in place, the collusion still happens with reliable success, often above 90%.

119
00:20:59.650 --> 00:21:02.969
joan velja: Why is this problematic, some might ask? Well.

120
00:21:03.420 --> 00:21:09.789
joan velja: as an intuition pump, I often think that, like, if you have 100%,

121
00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:13.670
joan velja: Payload success communication in one bit.

122
00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:23.700
joan velja: then all you need is sort of a long enough timetable for this to keep on going. And so, yeah, this is, this is very much a problem.

123
00:21:26.260 --> 00:21:31.580
joan velja: So what has happened in the two years since we wrote this paper?

124
00:21:31.830 --> 00:21:43.719
joan velja: Agents did end up communicating past their monitors in real training runs and deployments, and not just in our setup. But almost none of it went through the sort of monitor channeled and

125
00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:47.639
joan velja: flowed into steganography. But actually, they went around it.

126
00:21:49.690 --> 00:21:58.870
joan velja: And so, what I have in this slide is the two ways in which one can think an AI or a team of AIs can beat a monitor channel.

127
00:21:59.140 --> 00:22:05.679
joan velja: What we did see, in our work is through the channel itself, so by means of technography.

128
00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:14.649
joan velja: And this needs a shared code, which is agreed between the sender and the receiver, oftentimes without even needing to discuss it.

129
00:22:15.880 --> 00:22:27.530
joan velja: A cover text that still does the task and still doesn't flag the monitor, and a payload that survives, sort of, paraphrasing or whatever kind of, tampering that we might do to the stegatext.

130
00:22:29.250 --> 00:22:33.850
joan velja: What has indeed hap… what has instead happened, is…

131
00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:38.580
joan velja: That the agents, in this case, stylized, the sender and the receiver.

132
00:22:38.770 --> 00:22:42.009
joan velja: have learned to go around the monitor channel.

133
00:22:42.130 --> 00:22:53.150
joan velja: And this is much simpler, because it just needs a side channel, so any path that wasn't really, intent or meant for the communication to happen.

134
00:22:56.800 --> 00:23:05.769
joan velja: And, yeah, I've gathered here a bunch of, transcripts from the OpenAI misalignment reports, which have been,

135
00:23:06.000 --> 00:23:10.079
joan velja: published over the course of the summer and after the Hugging Face hack.

136
00:23:10.430 --> 00:23:14.610
joan velja: And what you can see is that the very… like, the… the…

137
00:23:14.720 --> 00:23:25.660
joan velja: most prominent way in which the models, coordinate with each other is often through plain text on unwatch channels, and that's the, sort of, topmost row.

138
00:23:27.610 --> 00:23:34.810
joan velja: On the bottom row, instead, we have models that use common encodings, Base64, chunking, and

139
00:23:34.960 --> 00:23:47.239
joan velja: We'll hold onto that thought for a second, but these are sort of somewhat more sophisticated means of going around the channel with still a compression sort of method, or…

140
00:23:47.450 --> 00:23:48.140
joan velja: Sure.

141
00:23:50.850 --> 00:23:59.469
joan velja: Encoding, as you saw in the bottom row in the slide before, only appears when coordination between the sender and the receiver is free.

142
00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:02.330
joan velja: So, whenever the sender is the receiver.

143
00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:12.410
joan velja: the agent encodes data into CI, checking messages and reading them back to itself, or compressing a file, and chunking it through error messages and reassembling it.

144
00:24:14.010 --> 00:24:25.569
joan velja: The encoding, on the other hand, could also be sort of a standard, namely, as I said earlier, Base64, chunking, or the DNS hostname format. Nobody really has to agree on anything new.

145
00:24:26.000 --> 00:24:29.599
joan velja: And, As a sort of,

146
00:24:30.590 --> 00:24:49.779
joan velja: further thought, the encoding often is a comment that an agent leaves in its own code after splitting a researcher's, sort of, token into pieces. The adversary tries to beat it with an automatic signal scanner, but it wasn't really hiding anything in particular. It's just a cover channel problem rather than a stenography problem.

147
00:24:53.230 --> 00:24:57.389
joan velja: So you might agree with me,

148
00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:05.330
joan velja: For now, that… then the coordination between the sender and the receiver, whenever these two sort of instances are uncommunicating.

149
00:25:05.560 --> 00:25:12.200
joan velja: to be the bottleneck. And this has been, hinted at for many…

150
00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:22.920
joan velja: for some time by now, in the sense that we tried to, like, make a claim about this in our 2024 work, but, like, there's been follow-ups that still,

151
00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:26.510
joan velja: Seemed to reinforce this, this notion.

152
00:25:30.080 --> 00:25:45.989
joan velja: And so I would like to, like, think speculatively into, okay, how do we flip the prior, or how does the prior itself even flip? Namely, if going around is cheaper, when should we expect agents to go through the communication channel that is monitored again?

153
00:25:46.790 --> 00:25:52.720
joan velja: Because… I would argue that that's where our results from 2 years ago become relevant again.

154
00:25:54.910 --> 00:26:03.979
joan velja: So I think that three conditions sort of push the agents back through the monitor channel. First, the side channels are closed, or are very difficult to reach.

155
00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:21.119
joan velja: the standard fix in every incident report that I showed earlier, but even that is being circulated often these days, is that, we're gonna fix sandboxes, and we're gonna make them so that, you know, agents cannot exfiltrate them or talk to each other through unintended means.

156
00:26:22.100 --> 00:26:33.249
joan velja: Second, coordination between the sender and the receiver is cheap. Either the sender is itself the receiver, or both sort of share the same weights.

157
00:26:34.350 --> 00:26:38.380
joan velja: And third, this optimization pressure lands on the channel itself.

158
00:26:38.570 --> 00:26:46.189
joan velja: The sequence of results since 2024 has been, that, for example, training against the chain of thought monitor produces obfuscation.

159
00:26:46.190 --> 00:27:03.030
joan velja: that penalizing a specific string produces encoding substitutes. And this has been worked by one of my co-authors as a follow-up to ours. Output-only supervision obfuscates the CFT, and as of this week, misspecified rewards alone are enough to degrade monitorability.

160
00:27:09.360 --> 00:27:15.869
joan velja: And so, I'd like to sort of close this Steganography sort of build-up to…

161
00:27:16.070 --> 00:27:20.339
joan velja: A speculative, maybe, thought, which hopefully gets you thinking about

162
00:27:20.460 --> 00:27:26.740
joan velja: where we might expect this to come along again. And so, what I have in this slide is

163
00:27:27.330 --> 00:27:42.300
joan velja: how and where should we expect Steganography to appear again? And so, I divide between three, sort of, possible modes, namely within one model, sender and receiver being the same weights, and they just have to, like, loop around a…

164
00:27:42.420 --> 00:27:47.189
joan velja: Monitoring channel, or whatever. Copies of the same weights, and different models.

165
00:27:48.020 --> 00:27:58.950
joan velja: So for the first case, whenever the sender and the receiver are the same weights, the steganography that we've observed so far is that the Stegonographic chain of thought sort of,

166
00:27:59.550 --> 00:28:12.309
joan velja: Under pressure supervision becomes… process supervision, sorry, becomes stegographically encoded, or that Steganographic reasoning learned when the covering task makes hiding convenient happens.

167
00:28:13.320 --> 00:28:29.380
joan velja: whenever we have the copies, two copies of the same weights, what we've observed so far is our 2024 in-context RL runs. Namely, Claude 3.5 Sonnet, as both the sender and the receiver, learns a collusion scheme that, bypasses a paraphraser.

168
00:28:31.080 --> 00:28:49.139
joan velja: What we could expect, and this is something that, you know, people might want to look into, is that stagnography could also happen by means of shelling points, namely two weights that share the same set of priors and biases, could zero-shot coordinate on

169
00:28:49.230 --> 00:28:54.099
joan velja: Scheme without any… Coordination whatsoever.

170
00:28:54.760 --> 00:29:12.999
joan velja: And the most expensive and complicated to coordinate Stegonographic lens scheme is whenever the models are different. And so, what I posit is that they need to learn code in the open, and they need to do this by virtue of optimization pressure against the monitoring channel.

171
00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:27.150
joan velja: And so at the bottom here, the TLDR of this slide is that we might expect hidden encodings to first arise in one model, and then between copies of the same model, and last, in terms of complexity, between different models themselves.

172
00:29:27.240 --> 00:29:36.550
joan velja: every result that I showed above is, sort of from the lab, namely in vitro. In the wild, though so far agents have gone around the channels.

173
00:29:38.260 --> 00:29:43.949
